<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<!-- When creating a new activity, make a copy of this file with appropriate name -->
<worksheet xml:id="recitation-Laplace-derivative">
  <title>Derivative of the Transform</title>

  <!-- Optional introduction -->
  <introduction>
    <p>
      We've seen a rule showing how the Laplace transform interacts with a derivative of the input function:
      <md>
        <mrow> \L\{f'(t)\} = sF(s) - f(0) </mrow>
      </md>
      But what's the interaction with a derivative of the transform? It turns out there is a nice formula:
    </p>

    <theorem>
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m> F'(s) = -\L\{t f(t)\}. </m>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </theorem>
  </introduction>


  <page>
    <exercise workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          For any function <m>g(s, t)</m>, it is a fact (part of the <term>Leibniz integral rule</term>) that:
          <md>
            <mrow> \frac{d}{ds} \int_a^b g(s, t)\ dt = \int_a^b \frac{\partial}{\partial s}(g(s, t))\ dt. </mrow>
          </md>
          Use this fact to prove the theorem.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Use the theorem to find a formula for <m>F''(s)</m>.
          What about <m>F'''(s)</m>? What about <m>F^{(n)}(s)</m>?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
  </page>


  <page>
    <exercise>
      <task workspace="1in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Find the first several derivatives of <m>\frac{1}{s + 1}</m>.
            Write a formula for the <m>n</m>th derivative.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>


      <task workspace="1in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Find <m>\L^{-1}\left\{\frac{1}{(s + 1)^4}\right\}.</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>
    </exercise>
  </page>
</worksheet>